.TH WSTATTEST 1
.SH NAME
wstattest \- check that a 9P wstat's "do not change" sentinel is honoured
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wstattest
.SH DESCRIPTION
A 9P
.B wstat
field of
.B "all ones"
means
.I "do not change this field" .
.I Wstattest
makes a pipe, records its mode, sends a
.B wstat
built from
.BR Sys->nulldir ,
and requires the mode to be unchanged. It then sends a
.B wstat
carrying a real mode and requires that one to be applied.
.PP
.B "Both arms are needed."
A device that ignored
.I every
.B wstat
would pass the first check on its own, so the second is what makes the
first mean anything.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/wstattest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR styxdirtest (1),
.IR stat (2)
.SH BUGS
.B "It tests one device."
A pipe reaches
.BR emu/port/devpipe.c .
Six others carried the same defect \(em
.BR devcmd ,
.BR devip ,
.BR devprog ,
.BR devsrv ,
.B devssl
and
.B Nt/deveia
\(em and none is exercised here. Each was fixed by inspection against
the one that is measured.
.PP
.B "It tests mode and nothing else."
.BR atime ,
.BR mtime ,
.B gid
and
.B length
have their own sentinel paths and are untouched by this test.
.PP
.B "It cannot run on a kernel."
The defect is specific to a host
.B emu ,
where
.B ulong
is 64 bits while
.B Dir.mode
is a
.BR u32 ,
so
.B ~0UL
promotes the field and the comparison is always true.
.B os/inferno/port
carries the identical line and is
.I correct,
because kencc's
.B long
is 32 bits \(em which is why this survived: the kernel is what gets
tested. A pass here says nothing about any board, and a board cannot
reproduce the failure.
.PP
.B "And emu exits 0 whether a Limbo command raises or not,"
so a script that checks only the exit status will call a failing run a
pass. Read the output. This is the mirror of a program that prints
success and exits non-zero, and it is why the summary line names the
counts.
